WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.840
The attack surface is every place where a user, system, file, browser, API client, administrator, integration, or background process can influence the application.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.660
Routes and forms are obvious examples, but they are only the beginning.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:28.400
API endpoints, authentication flows, password reset pages, session cookies, file uploads, admin panels, webhook receivers, search fields, reporting tools, and internal management interfaces all deserve review.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.780
Anything that accepts input or makes a decision can become security-relevant.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.160
Client-side code is part of the surface even when enforcement must happen on the server.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:48.900
JavaScript, browser storage, hidden fields, and front-end route checks can shape the user experience, but users can inspect, modify, replay, and bypass client-side behavior.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.080
Third-party integrations also expand the surface.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:01:04.300
A payment processor, analytics script, identity provider, email service, package, or embedded widget can affect security, privacy, availability, or the trust model of the application.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.760
Deployment configuration completes the picture.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:22.420
Debug settings, cloud storage permissions, container images, environment variables, CORS policies, TLS configuration, security headers, logging destinations, and secret handling can create vulnerabilities even when the application code looks reasonable.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.640
Mapping the attack surface helps teams decide where to focus review and testing.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:38.380
It also prevents blind spots, because security issues often appear where two features meet: a file upload feeding a parser, an admin tool calling an API, or a public route using an internal helper.
